{'id': '277ab741-eba4-3c2f-3a27-d975e4fb63a5', 'name': 'generate', 'error': None, 'result': [('generation', {'prefix': 'The N-Queens problem is a classic algorithmic problem where the goal is to place N queens on an N x N chessboard such that no two queens threaten each other. This solution uses a backtracking approach to explore all possible placements of queens, recursively checking if each placement is valid, and collecting all valid boards as solutions.', 'imports': '', 'code': 'def solve_n_queens(n):\n    def is_valid(board, row, col):\n        for i in range(row):\n            if board[i] == col or \\\n               board[i] - i == col - row or \\\n               board[i] + i == col + row:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def solve(board, row):\n        if row == n:\n            result.append(board[:])\n            return\n        for col in range(n):\n            if is_valid(board, row, col):\n                board[row] = col\n                solve(board, row + 1)\n\n    result = []\n    board = [-1] * n  # Initialize the board\n    solve(board, 0)\n    return result\n\n# Example of solving for 4 queens\nn = 4\nsolutions = solve_n_queens(n)\nprint(solutions)'}), ('messages', [AIMessage(content='The N-Queens problem is a classic algorithmic problem where the goal is to place N queens on an N x N chessboard such that no two queens threaten each other. This solution uses a backtracking approach to explore all possible placements of queens, recursively checking if each placement is valid, and collecting all valid boards as solutions. \n Imports:  \n Code: def solve_n_queens(n):\n    def is_valid(board, row, col):\n        for i in range(row):\n            if board[i] == col or \\\n               board[i] - i == col - row or \\\n               board[i] + i == col + row:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def solve(board, row):\n        if row == n:\n            result.append(board[:])\n            return\n        for col in range(n):\n            if is_valid(board, row, col):\n                board[row] = col\n                solve(board, row + 1)\n\n    result = []\n    board = [-1] * n  # Initialize the board\n    solve(board, 0)\n    return result\n\n# Example of solving for 4 queens\nn = 4\nsolutions = solve_n_queens(n)\nprint(solutions)', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={})]), ('iterations', 1)], 'interrupts': []}
